#
# ENGINE_PIN — the vouchfx engine version and commit this MCP server is pinned to.
#
# What it pins
# ------------
# The first line is  <version> <full-40-character-commit-sha>  — two
# space-separated fields, e.g. "v1.0.0-alpha.9 8c579ab4315cacba4066bc3f33dc24a19ca6c3d1".
# src/Vouchfx.Mcp/EnginePin.cs parses exactly this format.
#
# Unlike vouchfx-samples (which shallow-fetches and builds the engine from
# source at a pinned commit), vouchfx-mcp wraps the PUBLISHED `vouchfx` dotnet
# tool as a subprocess — it never builds the engine from source. The two
# fields therefore pin two different things:
#
#   - VERSION  is what the CLI handshake verifies against at run time: the MCP
#     server invokes the installed `vouchfx` tool, reads its reported version,
#     and refuses to proceed (or warns) if it does not match this line. This
#     is the field that actually gates behaviour.
#
#   - COMMIT SHA is the engine ref that the vendored artefacts this repo ships
#     — the JSON Schema, the generated language reference, and the recipe
#     library (vendored under vendored/ and drift-gated in CI) — are drift-gated against. CI
#     re-generates or diffs those artefacts against the engine source at this
#     exact commit so a silent drift between "what the docs/schema say" and
#     "what the pinned CLI version actually does" is caught mechanically
#     rather than discovered by a user.
#
# A full SHA is required, not a short SHA or a branch/tag name, for the same
# reproducibility reason as every other fleet satellite's ENGINE_PIN: a moving
# ref defeats the point of a pin.
#
# If the SHA above is ever the all-zeros placeholder, EnginePin.Load/Parse
# will refuse to run against it (see EnginePinTests.cs).
#
# When to advance it
# -------------------
# Bump this file when:
#   - a new `vouchfx` pre-release/release is published on NuGet.org that this
#     MCP server should target (most common reason — this repo tracks
#     published tool versions, not engine milestones directly);
#   - the vendored JSON Schema / language reference / recipes need to move to
#     a newer engine commit (e.g. a schema field or step family was added);
#   - the engine's v1.x SDK/event contract freeze advances and this server's
#     event-stream parsing should validate against the new baseline.
#
# How to advance it
# ------------------
# Two easy ways to find the commit SHA for a given tag/release WITHOUT cloning:
#   - GitHub: open the release/tag page (e.g.
#     github.com/tomas-rampas/vouchfx/releases/tag/v1.0.0-alpha.9) and click
#     the short-SHA link under the tag name to reach the full SHA.
#   - `gh api repos/tomas-rampas/vouchfx/commits/<tag> --jq .sha` — PREFER
#     THIS. It resolves straight to the commit SHA for either kind of tag,
#     with no peeling to get wrong.
#   - `git ls-remote --tags https://github.com/tomas-rampas/vouchfx '<tag>*'`
#     — GOTCHA, corrected 2026-08-10 after measuring the real refs. Two
#     things the previous wording got wrong:
#       (a) vouchfx's tags are MIXED, not uniformly annotated. Measured:
#           v1.0.0-rc.1 and v1.0.0-rc.4 are ANNOTATED (tag object != commit),
#           v1.0.0-rc.3 is LIGHTWEIGHT (the ref IS the commit). So you cannot
#           assume either shape — check, do not extrapolate.
#       (b) the bare `<tag>` pattern prints only ONE line even for an
#           annotated tag, because the peeled ref is literally named
#           `refs/tags/<tag>^{}` and does not match the pattern `<tag>`.
#           Following the old recipe verbatim on v1.0.0-rc.4 yields
#           9775354b… — the TAG OBJECT's SHA, i.e. exactly the wrong value.
#           Use the trailing-glob form above so the `^{}` line is emitted too.
#     Rule regardless of shape: ENGINE_PIN holds the COMMIT SHA. If a `^{}`
#     line is present, that is the one; if the tag is lightweight, the single
#     line already is the commit.
#     Cross-check the result for free: the installed CLI's informational
#     version embeds the engine commit — `vouchfx --version` on the pinned
#     build prints `<version>+<commit sha>`, which must equal this file's
#     first line field-for-field.
#
# Steps:
#   1. Pick the new `vouchfx` version to target and its corresponding engine
#      commit SHA (peeled, per the gotcha above).
#   2. Replace the first line of this file with the new "<version> <sha>" pair.
#   3. Re-validate: re-run the packaged-CLI handshake and the vendored
#      artefact drift check (see vendored/README.md) against the new pin.
#   3b. Reconcile shared dependency pins with the engine at the new commit: read the engine's
#      Directory.Packages.props at that SHA and match YamlDotNet exactly (the csproj policy —
#      fleet consistency on YAML scalar resolution is a correctness property). Added 2026-09-12
#      after the 18.1.0 divergence below survived 46 days because the check hung off "when
#      advancing YamlDotNet" — a trigger nobody hits on a pin bump. A guard test also asserts the
#      YamlDotNet version the vendored schema itself names equals the loaded library's version.
#   4. Run the graceful-teardown LIVE DRILL against the newly-pinned CLI and
#      CONFIRM IT PASSES before proceeding (see docs/validation/graceful-teardown-drill.md
#      for the exact procedure). This is MANDATORY: the MCP's GracefulShutdownGrace (35s)
#      is only safe while the engine's own teardown budget stays ≤ ~30s. The drill is
#      the only gate that catches a budget divergence — if a future engine release grows
#      its teardown budget past 35s, the drill will fail empirically (engine self-exits
#      after the grace deadline, MCP force-kills, topology is orphaned) rather than
#      silently regressing to the old orphan-then-Ryuk-reaps behaviour.
#   5. Open a PR with the pin bump (plus any artefact regeneration the new version
#      requires) so the change is reviewable in isolation. Evidence of the passed drill
#      (e.g., a note in the PR description referencing the drill procedure and confirming
#      it passed) is recommended for transparency.
#
